## Proof

**Claim:** Yes. In a Noetherian commutative ring $R$, if $I \subseteq P$ with $P$ prime and $\sqrt{I}=P$, then there exists an integer $n\geq 1$ such that $P^n \subseteq I$.

**Proof.**

Since $R$ is Noetherian, every ideal is finitely generated. In particular the prime ideal $P$ is finitely generated; write
$$P = (p_1, p_2, \dots, p_m)$$
for some $p_1,\dots,p_m \in P$.

Because $\sqrt{I}=P$, each generator $p_i$ lies in $\sqrt{I}$, so by definition of the radical there exists a positive integer $k_i$ such that
$$p_i^{k_i} \in I, \qquad i=1,\dots,m.$$

Set
$$N := k_1 + k_2 + \cdots + k_m - m + 1.$$

The power $P^N$ is the ideal generated by all products of $N$ elements of $P$. Since $P=(p_1,\dots,p_m)$, the ideal $P^N$ is generated by all monomials
$$p_1^{a_1} p_2^{a_2} \cdots p_m^{a_m} \quad\text{with}\quad a_1+a_2+\cdots+a_m = N,\; a_i \ge 0.$$

Consider any such monomial. Suppose, for contradiction, that $a_i \le k_i - 1$ for every $i$. Then
$$N = \sum_{i=1}^m a_i \le \sum_{i=1}^m (k_i - 1) = (k_1+\cdots+k_m) - m = N - 1,$$
which is a contradiction. Hence for some index $j$ we have $a_j \ge k_j$.

Since $p_j^{k_j} \in I$ and $a_j \ge k_j$, we may write
$$p_j^{a_j} = p_j^{k_j} \cdot p_j^{a_j - k_j},$$
so $p_j^{a_j} \in I$ (as $I$ is an ideal). Therefore
$$p_1^{a_1}\cdots p_m^{a_m} = \bigl(p_j^{a_j}\bigr)\cdot \prod_{i\ne j} p_i^{a_i} \in I,$$
again because $I$ is an ideal.

Thus every generator of $P^N$ lies in $I$, giving $P^N \subseteq I$. Taking $n = N$ completes the proof. $\blacksquare$

